  "grammar_explanations": [
    {
      "slug": "some/any: affirmative and questions",
      "title_form": "Some y Any: afirmativo y preguntas",
      "title_target": "Some and Any: Affirmative and Questions",
      "description_md_form": "En inglés, **some** y **any** significan \"algo de\" o \"algunos\". Se usan con sustantivos contables e incontables.\n\n- **Some** se usa en **oraciones afirmativas**: `I want some water.`\n- **Any** se usa en **preguntas** y **negaciones**: `Do you have any coffee?` / `I don't have any sugar.`\n\n**Regla clave:** en preguntas, usa **any**, no some. Pero si ofreces algo, puedes usar some: `Do you want some tea?`\n\n**Contables vs. incontables:**\n- **Much** para incontables: `How much sugar?`\n- **Many** para contables: `How many cups?`\n\n**All** significa \"todo\": `I drink all the juice.`",
      "description_md_target": "In English, **some** and **any** mean \"a little\" or \"some\". They are used with countable and uncountable nouns.\n\n- **Some** is used in **affirmative sentences**: `I want some water.`\n- **Any** is used in **questions** and **negations**: `Do you have any coffee?` / `I don't have any sugar.`\n\n**Key rule:** in questions, use **any**, not some. But if you offer something, you can use some: `Do you want some tea?`\n\n**Countable vs. uncountable:**\n- **Much** for uncountable: `How much sugar?`\n- **Many** for countable: `How many cups?`\n\n**All** means \"everything\": `I drink all the juice.`",
      "grammar_structure": "Affirmative: Subject + verb + some + noun\nQuestion: Do/Does + subject + verb + any + noun?\nNegative: Subject + don't/doesn't + verb + any + noun",
      "conjugation_table": {
        "name": "some/any usage",
        "headers": [
          "Tipo de oración",
          "Contable",
          "Incontable"
        ],
        "rows": [
